import CombinatorialGroupTheory_Magnus_2004.Basic

-- Declarations for this item will be appended below by the statement pipeline.

universe u

section

variable {F : Type u} [Group F] [IsFreeGroup F]

open Subgroup

/- Layer triage:
- `source-facing`: a primitive element `p` of a free group and an element `q : F`, with `p`
  lying in the singleton normal closure generated by `q`.
- `core/canonical`: the chapter owner predicate `IsPrimitiveElement`, together with
  `Subgroup.normalClosure` for singleton normal closures and `IsConj` for conjugacy.
- `bridge/view`: a chosen basis element `basis x` is only a witness for `IsPrimitiveElement p`
  through `FreeGroupBasis.isPrimitiveElement`.
- Domain sampling:
  1. `IsPrimitiveElement` from `CombinatorialGroupTheory.Basic` is the project owner for
     “belongs to some free basis”.
  2. `Subgroup.normalClosure` is mathlib's owner construction for the normal closure of a subset.
  3. `IsConj` is mathlib's owner relation for conjugacy in a group.
  4. `FreeGroupBasis.isPrimitiveElement` remains the canonical bridge from a chosen basis element
     to `IsPrimitiveElement`, so this file does not need a parallel basis-element public wrapper.
- Primitive vs. derived:
  the primitive public data are the element `p`, its owner-side primitivity hypothesis, the
  element `q`, and the membership `p ∈ normalClosure {q}`; any basis/index witness for
  `p` is bridge data rather than part of the main theorem surface. -/
-- The numbered proposition is exposed directly at the owner level `IsPrimitiveElement`.

/-- Proposition 2-5-14: if the normal closure of `q` in a free group contains a primitive element
`p`, then `q` is conjugate to `p` or to `p⁻¹`. -/
-- Proof sketch: this is Magnus's theorem for a one-relator normal closure containing a primitive
-- element. Since `p` is primitive, quotienting by its normal closure removes exactly one free
-- generator. The hypothesis that `p` already lies in the normal closure of `q` forces the
-- one-relator quotient by `q` to have the same primitive-killing behavior, and Magnus's
-- conjugacy theorem then identifies `q` with `p` up to conjugacy and inversion.
theorem conjugate_or_conjugate_inv_of_mem_normalClosure_of_isPrimitiveElement
    {p q : F} (hp : IsPrimitiveElement p) (hmem : p ∈ normalClosure {q}) :
    IsConj q p ∨ IsConj q p⁻¹ := sorry

/-- If the normal closure of `q` contains a primitive element, then `q` is primitive. -/
-- Proof sketch: apply Proposition `2-5-14` to identify `q` up to conjugacy and inversion with
-- the given primitive element `p`. Primitivity is preserved under inversion and under inner
-- automorphisms, so either conjugacy alternative yields `IsPrimitiveElement q`.
theorem isPrimitiveElement_of_mem_normalClosure_of_isPrimitiveElement
    {p q : F} (hp : IsPrimitiveElement p) (hmem : p ∈ normalClosure {q}) :
    IsPrimitiveElement q := by
  rcases conjugate_or_conjugate_inv_of_mem_normalClosure_of_isPrimitiveElement hp hmem with
    hconj | hconj
  · exact IsPrimitiveElement.of_isConj hp hconj
  · exact IsPrimitiveElement.of_isConj hp.inv hconj

end
